<h2>Why the certificate became the battlefield</h2>
<p>Chapter V of the MSMED Act gives suppliers remedies strong enough — statutory interest at three times the bank rate, compounded monthly, and a 75% pre-deposit barrier against challenges — that buyers&rsquo; most economical defence is often jurisdictional: deny that the claimant is a &ldquo;supplier&rdquo; at all. Since Udyam registration is how supplier status is evidenced, the attacks cluster around the certificate&rsquo;s timing, truthfulness and continuity.</p>

<h2>The four recurring attacks</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Timing.</strong> &ldquo;Registration post-dates the supplies.&rdquo; The Silpi Industries principle confines the Act&rsquo;s benefits to supplies made after registration — so the claim must be segmented, not abandoned: invoices after the registration date remain protected.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Truthfulness.</strong> &ldquo;Investment or turnover was misdeclared.&rdquo; Udyam runs on self-declaration cross-verified against PAN, GST and income-tax data, so a buyer alleging inflation needs material, not suspicion — and the allegation does not suspend the Council&rsquo;s jurisdiction while it is investigated.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Continuity.</strong> &ldquo;The certificate lapsed or was cancelled.&rdquo; Administrative lapses (non-updation of ITR/GST data) are curable and usually operate prospectively; the supplier&rsquo;s rights are tested as on the dates of supply and reference.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Reclassification.</strong> &ldquo;The enterprise outgrew MSME status.&rdquo; Growth beyond thresholds changes protection prospectively; it does not forfeit interest already accrued on protected supplies.</div>
</div>

<h2>Period-wise segmentation: the drafting discipline</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Supply period</th><th>Status on invoice date</th><th>Chapter V protection</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Before registration</td><td>Unregistered</td><td>Not available (Silpi); ordinary contractual/commercial remedies apply</td></tr>
  <tr><td>After registration, within thresholds</td><td>Registered MSME</td><td>Full: Sections 15–18, interest and Council jurisdiction</td></tr>
  <tr><td>After upward reclassification</td><td>No longer micro/small</td><td>Doubtful for new supplies; accrued claims for earlier supplies survive</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A statement of claim built on this grid — with the Udyam certificate, its history printout, and invoice-wise annexures — pre-empts the buyer&rsquo;s jurisdictional theatre and lets the Council move to the real questions of supply, acceptance and non-payment.</p>

<h2>Protective steps for suppliers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Keep the registration current.</strong> Update turnover and investment data each year; a lapsed certificate invites avoidable objections even where rights have accrued.</p>
  <p><strong>Preserve the paper for each period.</strong> Download and archive the certificate and classification as they stood at each financial year — the portal shows current status, but disputes concern past dates.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer cancellation on the correct forum.</strong> A certificate cancelled or questioned administratively should be defended through the Udyam grievance route, while the Council reference proceeds on the record as it stood at supply.</p>
  <p><strong>Buyers, too, should segment.</strong> A blanket &ldquo;no jurisdiction&rdquo; plea fails where even part of the supplies is protected; credible defences concede the protected window and contest the rest.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer discusses recurring registration issues in MSMED Act claims for general information. It is not legal advice; the effect of any registration defect depends on the supply timeline and the record before the Council.</div>
